## Complete Implementation

Here's how to implement the `read_header_and_skip_body` function using the Hexi library:

```cpp
#include <hexi/binary_stream.h>
#include <hexi/buffer_adaptor.h>
#include <hexi/endian.h>
#include <span>
#include <cstdint>

struct MessageHeader {
    uint16_t message_type;
    uint32_t body_length;
    uint32_t footer;
};

MessageHeader read_header_and_skip_body(const std::span<const uint8_t> buffer) {
    // Create a buffer adaptor to wrap the std::span
    hexi::buffer_adaptor adaptor(buffer);

    // Create a binary stream for reading
    hexi::binary_stream stream(adaptor);

    MessageHeader header;

    // Read message_type (uint16_t)
    stream >> header.message_type;

    // Read body_length (uint32_t)
    stream >> header.body_length;

    // Skip the body data using the skip() function
    stream.skip(header.body_length);

    // Read footer (uint32_t)
    stream >> header.footer;

    return header;
}
```

**Key dependencies used:**
- `hexi::buffer_adaptor` - wraps the `std::span<const uint8_t>` buffer
- `hexi::binary_stream` - provides `operator>>` for reading uint16_t and uint32_t, and `skip()` method
- `skip(length)` method - skips over the specified number of bytes in the buffer without reading them

The library handles:
- Bounds checking automatically
- Endianness conversion (defaults to native endianness)
- Error handling through stream state checking

